"A publish-subscribe diagram shows independent consumers receiving messages through channels or subscriptions rather than direct producer knowledge.\n\nUse when: Review event fan-out and subscriber isolation. Explain channel contracts and independent consumer responsibilities.\n\nAvoid when: Exactly one worker should handle each queued task.\n\nVisual grammar: Publishers connect to named channels, topics, or brokers. Separate subscriptions encode independent delivery to consumer groups. Message labels name events and distinguish filters or routing rules.\n\nBuild: Identify publishers, event contracts, and interested consumer groups. Show subscription boundaries rather than one ambiguous shared consumer line. Add delivery, retry, and dead-letter behavior where relevant. Place publishers left, channels centrally, and subscribers right with dedicated fan-out corridors.\n\nCheck: Independent subscriptions are distinguished from competing consumers. Ordering and delivery guarantees are scoped to the actual broker. Observed subscriptions and proposed consumers are labeled accurately. Topic names, filters, and fan-out arrows remain separate.\n\nPitfalls: Claiming that asynchronous delivery removes all coupling. Using a single queue symbol to imply every subscriber receives every message.\n\nExample: OrderPlaced is published once and delivered through independent Billing and Analytics subscriptions.\n\nAlternatives: Competing-consumer diagram, Stream processing topology."
